WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.480
A mouse has a nerve injury, and a touch makes its paw pull away.

2
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:14.200
Is that the pain? A withdrawal reflex is cheap to measure.

3
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.200
It is also easy to mistake for pain.

4
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.020
An unpleasant experience is a different measurement.

5
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.100
Most pain assays record the first.

6
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.640
So where does the second one live?

7
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:31.473
Corder and colleagues asked a sharp question.

8
00:00:32.373 --> 00:00:35.833
Does a defined set of neurons carry the second measurement?

9
00:00:36.693 --> 00:00:39.253
They worked in male mice with a nerve injury.

10
00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:41.733
Two endpoints can be measured.

11
00:00:42.553 --> 00:00:44.213
One is the withdrawal reflex.

12
00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:49.633
The other is affective-motivational behaviour, such as escape and avoidance.

13
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:56.897
First, in male mice, they imaged principal neurons in the basolateral amygdala.

14
00:00:57.717 --> 00:01:00.477
They imaged the same mice in repeated sessions.

15
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:05.777
They identified an ensemble, a set of cells that respond together.

16
00:01:06.517 --> 00:01:08.617
It responded to noxious stimuli.

17
00:01:09.197 --> 00:01:11.457
And it encoded their negative valence.

18
00:01:11.457 --> 00:01:15.517
Valence means how bad or good a stimulus is.

19
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:19.010
Then came the nerve injury in these mice.

20
00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.270
After the injury, innocuous stimuli also recruited that ensemble.

21
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.630
An innocuous stimulus is one that is normally not painful.

22
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:34.490
So the cells that encoded negative valence were now recruited by innocuous stimuli.

23
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:39.213
This tested whether the two endpoints could be pulled apart.

24
00:01:40.293 --> 00:01:43.273
Next, they tagged the active neurons genetically.

25
00:01:43.973 --> 00:01:46.393
This used activity-dependent tagging.

26
00:01:47.353 --> 00:01:51.413
Then they silenced the tagged ensemble in nerve-injured male mice.

27
00:01:52.153 --> 00:01:55.113
The affective-motivational pain behaviours fell.

28
00:01:55.913 --> 00:01:58.233
The measured withdrawal reflexes were preserved.

29
00:01:58.853 --> 00:02:02.773
The paw still pulled away, but the escape and avoidance fell away.

30
00:02:02.773 --> 00:02:05.033
The two endpoints came apart.

31
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:07.567
Now read the scope.

32
00:02:08.347 --> 00:02:13.247
This is male mice, a nerve injury model, and the behaviours the authors chose.

33
00:02:13.927 --> 00:02:16.047
A mouse gives no verbal report.

34
00:02:16.827 --> 00:02:22.567
So the result separates two measurable endpoints, in one species and one assay.

35
00:02:23.207 --> 00:02:27.147
It does not show that unpleasantness is made in the basolateral amygdala.

36
00:02:27.827 --> 00:02:31.627
It does not show that silencing it abolishes sensing of the stimulus.

37
00:02:31.947 --> 00:02:34.147
It says nothing about other regions.

38
00:02:34.147 --> 00:02:41.047
Keep this rule. A withdrawal reflex is not a measure of how unpleasant a stimulus is.
